<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The application's own decision letter: what an organisation that has not sent us its own gets.

  It is a file, not code. An organisation changes its wording by uploading a .pdxdoc of its own
  (POST api/letter-templates) and nothing is deployed; this one is the declared fallback, and the
  reason a new organisation has a letter on its first day.

  ⚠️ ONE template, several languages. The text comes through `t:` — the application's translation
  keys, resolved for the applicant's language — rather than from literals here, which is what keeps
  this from becoming one file per language. The dates go through `date:`, so they follow the same
  culture as the words around them: a letter that says "Decisa il" and then writes 2026-09-18 the
  American way is two decisions disagreeing inside one paragraph.

  Every {{ … }} is an expression over the named roots the application passes: case, applicant and
  organization. A property that is not there is NOT an error — the expression resolves to null and the
  data context records a TemplateWarning — so a template that asks for something nobody provides makes
  a letter with a hole in it and no complaint. Reading those warnings is the application's job.
-->
<document title="{{ t:letter.decision.title }}" page-size="A4" margin="25">
  <page>
    <import src="header.pdxdoc" />

    <heading level="1">{{ t:letter.decision.title }}</heading>

    <text>{{ t:letter.decision.case(number=case.number, subject=case.subject) }}</text>
    <text>{{ t:letter.decision.applicant(name=applicant.name) }}</text>
    <text>{{ t:letter.decision.outcome(when=case.decidedOn | date:"D", outcome=case.outcome) }}</text>

    <hr thickness="0.4" />

    <text>{{ t:letter.decision.appeal(organization=organization.name) }}</text>
  </page>
</document>
